Year = {2014},
Volume = {53},
Number = {42},
Pages = {11236-11241},
Month = {OCT 13},
Abstract = {Cyclic disulfide-rich peptides have exceptional stability and are
   promising frameworks for drug design. We were interested in obtaining
   X-ray structures of these peptides to assist in drug design
   applications, but disulfide-rich peptides can be notoriously difficult
   to crystallize. To overcome this limitation, we chemically synthesized
   the L- and D-forms of three prototypic cyclic disulfide-rich peptides:
   SFTI-1 (14-mer with one disulfide bond), cVc1.1 (22-mer with two
   disulfide bonds), and kB1 (29-mer with three disulfide bonds) for
   racemic crystallization studies. Facile crystal formation occurred from
   a racemic mixture of each peptide, giving structures solved at
   resolutions from 1.25 angstrom to 1.9 angstrom. Additionally, we
   obtained the quasi-racemic structures of two mutants of kB1, {[}G6A]kB1,
   and {[}V25A]kB1, which were solved at a resolution of 1.25 angstrom and
   2.3 angstrom, respectively. The racemic crystallography approach appears
   to have broad utility in the structural biology of cyclic peptides.},
