Year = {2012},
Volume = {38},
Number = {1},
Pages = {1-9},
Month = {JAN},
Abstract = {Antimicrobial peptides (AMPs) are natural antibiotics produced by all
   living organisms to fight pathogens. They are important effector
   molecules of the immune system both in animals and plants. AMPs are
   diverse in structure and mode of action. Based on the homology of amino
   acid sequences and 3D structures several AMP families have been
   distinguished. They are defensins, thionins, lipid transfer proteins,
   hevein- and knottin-like peptides, and cyclotides. AMPs display
   broad-spectrum antimicrobial activity and thus show promise for the
   development of disease-resistant crops by genetic engineering and for
   the production of new-generation drugs. In this paper, the properties of
   the main AMP families (defensins and hevein-like peptides) and of new
   4-Cys plant AMP family are reviewed.},
